The iOS 27.2 beta that Apple released today adds a feature that reminds you it's your anniversary when you call someone, as long as you've added the date in the Contacts app.<br/>
<br/>
<img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1065607" src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/ios272anniversary.jpg" alt="" width="1400" height="788" /><br/>
On the date of your anniversary, when you go to call the person, you'll see a reminder that it's an important day so you don't forget to say something. The anniversary alert uses Call Context, a feature Apple added in <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-27/">iOS 27</a>.<br/>
<br/>
Call Context brings up information from emails and other sources when you place a phone call. If you place a call to an airline and you have an email with a flight confirmation number for that airline in the Mail app, it surfaces that confirmation number so you don't have to go digging for it.<br/>
<br/>
In &zwnj;iOS 27&zwnj;, Call Context works with birthdays, and it shows a fireworks animation when you're placing a call. iOS 27.2 adds anniversaries, but unfortunately, it doesn't come with a new animation.<br/>
<br/>
The Call Context anniversary option draws from your Contacts app. To add an anniversary to a contact, open the Contacts app and select their name. Tap "Edit" in the corner, then tap "add date," and the first suggestion under date is anniversary.<br/>
<br/>
You can also ask <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI to add the anniversary date to the person's contact card if you have a device that supports <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-intelligence/">Apple Intelligence</a>.<br/>
<br/>
